now if you watched president biden's  inauguration yesterday  there were a few standout moments but  perhaps the one that  may stick in your mind was the inaugural  poet it's the youth poet laureate amanda  gorman who delivered that and at 22  she is the youngest person ever to  deliver  an inaugural poem a nation that  isn't broken but simply unfinished we  the successors of a country and the time  where a skinny black girl descended from  slaves  and raised by a single mother can dream  of becoming president only to find  herself reciting  for one well let's talk to  lutheran williams head of the new roads  road school which  amanda gorman attended first and  foremost  you must be so proud well i'm i'm nearly  speechless i mean  it's hard to put into words how proud i  am um of what she's been able to do  it seems a little unfair mr williams  just to call her a poet  because i mean she's clearly a  performance poet  which is a i mean it's a whole different  skill set  yes she is a performance poet i think  that amanda  puts her whole soul in all of her  emotions and conveying  the depths of her poetry and it is  very much her unique artistic creation  that is very individualized i mean it  was a  you know in many respects a very sort of  you don't want to say it in a  patronizing way  but because she is so young it did seem  an incredibly mature piece  well i think it's really interesting you  know amanda attended  new road school from the time she was in  kindergarten and  the school was originally um  founded shortly after the rodney king  riots in 1995  and because the founders looked around  and they said there's not a  socioeconomically and racially  and culturally integrated school in los  angeles 40 years after brown versus the  board of education  so they decided to create this school  where the assumption would be  that all children have unlimited  potential  and that it is the um job of schools to  help them develop their unique gifts  and amanda got that invitation to  really find her voice and to think  deeply  critically and to really  i think have these profound insights  that she  could translate into these exquisite  works of art  did she show that level of potential  then at an earlier age i mean did was  she someone that you looked at  as as the head there and say well you  know we could really see things coming  from this girl  yes i will say that i mean she before  she left  um our school she became the first youth  laureate of los angeles  and then she became the first youth poet  laureate of  the west and then when she went to  harvard she became  the first youthport laureate of the  united states  amanda is incredibly driven and what's  beautiful about amanda is she's driven  to transform the world  is she a sort of very confident young  woman or or will she have been  i always think looking at even when she  stepped up to the podium yesterday i was  sort of had my  my heart in my mouth a little bit for  someone so young thinking  she must be terrified now  well it's interesting because you know  as i said i think one of the things that  you know i credit to some extent um the  school with is  our school gives the students an  invitation to be their authentic selves  and i think because  they are they are taught to be very  comfortable in themselves and they grow  up in an environment at the school  where the foundation is love and respect  and compassion  they really leave us with incredible  confidence and poise  and they are able to walk  their own road and to be unafraid in  many ways of what others think  i didn't think it was very interesting  that she you know she came out with a  piece that was  it reflected on the negative side of of  the last four years  in her view and in her opinion but not  not in an overly sort of  vicious way i mean it was she was very  much wanting to look at the positive  moving forward it really was a message  of hope i thought  yes i i think that's true i mean i think  that amanda's  poetry she offered a critical  well-informed perspective  of what had been happening in the  country  yet she was not cynical  and she gave the country the hope that  it could fulfill its promise  and i think that's very consistent with  the values that she grew up with  are you looking forward mr williams to  inviting her back to the school  to uh to give some sort of recital  well it's interesting amanda is still  very close to the school and amanda the  untold story is that amanda has an  extraordinary mother  and her mother remains in close contact  and amanda also has  a twin sister who is a brilliant  filmmaker who also started in  kindergarten  wow it's quite a family um  she's now turning into a real role model  though isn't she i mean the idea she  said in her own words the  the skinny black girl with a raised by a  single mother  doing what she's doing i mean that that  really does  pitch her as a role model for for  for particularly young black children  who  you know we know there's there's still a  lot of racial tension in the united  states may  look at the future with a more bleak  outlook  well i think that you know one of the  things that it makes me so proud about  amanda is that i think she  was able to attend our school and when  she attended our school she took  advantage of every opportunity  and she was able to really liberate her  potential  and i think amanda is the embodiment of  what's possible  when opportunity meets ambition  mr williams it is a pleasure to talk to  you this morning thank you very much  indeed
 

